      <section class="hero">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="tag">JPSC PRELIMS • EXAM PATTERN</div>

          <h1>
            JPSC Prelims Exam Pattern:
            <span>Know the Paper Before You Prepare.</span>
          </h1>

          <p class="hero-text">
            A clear guide to the JPSC Combined Civil Services Preliminary
            Examination structure—papers, marks, questions, duration, screening
            role, marking rules and the preparation strategy that follows from
            the pattern.
          </p>

          <div class="meta">
            <span>Updated: 16 August 2026</span>
            <span>•</span>
            <span>Habitat IAS</span>
            <span>•</span>
            <span>Prelims Strategy</span>
          </div>

          <div class="buttons">
            <a class="btn yellow" href="#pattern">See Pattern ↓</a>
            <a
              class="btn outline"
              href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/Syllabus.php"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Official JPSC Syllabus →
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="warning-section">
        <div class="container">
          <div class="warning">
            <strong>Current-cycle note:</strong>
            JPSC's official website currently lists the Combined Civil Services
            Examination-2025 under Advertisement No. 01/2026 and separately
            publishes the Combined Civil Services syllabus. This article uses
            the currently published framework and historical official rules
            where stated. Always check the latest advertisement, amendment and
            notice before the examination. citeturn0search6turn0search9
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="stats">
        <div class="container stats-grid">
          <div><b>02</b><span>Prelims papers</span><p>Two compulsory General Studies papers.</p></div>
          <div><b>400</b><span>Total marks</span><p>200 marks per paper under the published scheme.</p></div>
          <div><b>100</b><span>Questions / paper</span><p>Objective-type questions in each paper.</p></div>
          <div><b>2 HRS</b><span>Per paper</span><p>Time specified for each Preliminary paper.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content">
        <div class="container layout">
          <aside class="toc">
            <strong>ON THIS PAGE</strong>
            <a href="#pattern">Exam pattern</a>
            <a href="#table">Pattern at a glance</a>
            <a href="#paper1">Paper I</a>
            <a href="#paper2">Paper II</a>
            <a href="#marking">Marking rules</a>
            <a href="#screening">Screening & merit</a>
            <a href="#syllabus">Syllabus connection</a>
            <a href="#time">Time management</a>
            <a href="#pyq">PYQ strategy</a>
            <a href="#mock">Mock strategy</a>
            <a href="#mistakes">Common mistakes</a>
            <a href="#plan">Preparation plan</a>
            <a href="#faq">FAQs</a>
          </aside>

          <article>
            <p class="lead">
              Before collecting books, current-affairs PDFs and test series,
              understand the <strong>architecture of the examination</strong>.
              Your study plan should be designed around the actual paper
              structure, not around the number of books you can finish.
            </p>

            <h2 id="pattern">1. JPSC Prelims Exam Pattern at a Glance</h2>

            <p>
              JPSC's published Combined Civil Services framework specifies two
              compulsory Preliminary papers: <strong>General Studies
              Paper-I</strong> and <strong>General Studies Paper-II</strong>.
              Each paper carries 200 marks and has a two-hour duration. The
              published scheme specifies 100 objective-type questions in each
              paper, with each question carrying 2 marks. citeturn0search12turn0search13
            </p>

            <div class="pattern-cards">
              <div>
                <span>PAPER I</span>
                <strong>General Studies-I</strong>
                <b>200 marks</b>
                <small>100 objective questions • 2 hours</small>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>PAPER II</span>
                <strong>General Studies-II</strong>
                <b>200 marks</b>
                <small>100 objective questions • 2 hours</small>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>TOTAL</span>
                <strong>Prelims</strong>
                <b>400 marks</b>
                <small>Two compulsory papers</small>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula">
              <strong>100 questions × 2 marks = 200 marks per paper</strong>
              <span>Two papers × 200 marks = 400 marks in the Preliminary Examination.</span>
            </div>

            <h2 id="table">2. JPSC Prelims Pattern — Quick Table</h2>

            <div class="table">
              <div class="head"><span>Parameter</span><span>Published framework</span></div>
              <div><b>Stage</b><span>Preliminary Examination</span></div>
              <div><b>Number of papers</b><span>2 compulsory papers</span></div>
              <div><b>Paper I</b><span>General Studies Paper-I</span></div>
              <div><b>Paper II</b><span>General Studies Paper-II</span></div>
              <div><b>Marks per paper</b><span>200</span></div>
              <div><b>Total Preliminary marks</b><span>400</span></div>
              <div><b>Questions per paper</b><span>100 objective-type questions</span></div>
              <div><b>Marks per question</b><span>2</span></div>
              <div><b>Time per paper</b><span>2 hours</span></div>
              <div><b>Purpose</b><span>Screening test</span></div>
            </div>

            <p class="source-note">
              The table above follows the official JPSC 2024 advertisement and
              the 2021 examination rules published by JPSC. citeturn0search12turn0search13
            </p>

            <h2 id="paper1">3. General Studies Paper-I</h2>

            <p>
              The 2024 JPSC advertisement gives Paper-I a 200-mark, two-hour
              structure with 100 objective questions. It lists questions from
              History of India, Geography of India, Indian Polity and
              Governance, Economic and Sustainable Development, Science &
              Technology, Jharkhand-specific general awareness, national &
              international current events and General Knowledge. citeturn0search12
            </p>

            <div class="subject-map">
              <div><b>HISTORY</b><span>15 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>GEOGRAPHY</b><span>10 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>POLITY</b><span>10 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>ECONOMY</b><span>10 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>SCIENCE & TECH</b><span>15 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>JHARKHAND</b><span>10 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>CURRENT EVENTS</b><span>15 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
              <div><b>GENERAL KNOWLEDGE</b><span>15 questions in the published 2024 advertisement.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="rule">
              <strong>Important:</strong>
              These subject counts are from the published 2024 advertisement
              and should not automatically be treated as a guaranteed fixed
              distribution for every future cycle. The latest notification
              controls.
            </div>

            <h2 id="paper2">4. General Studies Paper-II</h2>

            <p>
              Paper-II is also a compulsory 200-mark Preliminary paper under the
              published framework. The 2021 official rules specify 100
              objective-type questions of 2 marks each and a two-hour duration
              for each Preliminary paper. citeturn0search13
            </p>

            <p>
              For preparation, do not rely on an old coaching summary for the
              exact Paper-II components. Open the current JPSC syllabus and
              advertisement and build your Paper-II checklist directly from the
              official document.
            </p>

            <div class="official-box">
              <strong>Best practice:</strong>
              Keep a saved copy of the latest official syllabus and notification
              in your study folder. If JPSC publishes an amendment or
              corrigendum, update your checklist immediately.
            </div>

            <h2 id="marking">5. JPSC Prelims Marking Rules</h2>

            <p>
              The official 2021 Combined Civil Services Examination Rules state
              that there is <strong>no negative marking for incorrect
              answers</strong>. They also state that if a candidate gives more
              than one answer to a question, it is treated as wrong and no marks
              are awarded; a blank question has no penalty. citeturn0search13
            </p>

            <div class="marking">
              <div class="positive"><b>✓</b><strong>Correct answer</strong><span>Marks awarded according to the applicable paper scheme.</span></div>
              <div><b>×</b><strong>Incorrect answer</strong><span>2021 rules: no negative marking.</span></div>
              <div><b>—</b><strong>Blank</strong><span>2021 rules: no penalty.</span></div>
              <div><b>!</b><strong>Multiple answers</strong><span>2021 rules: treated as wrong; no marks.</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="warning-box">
              <strong>Do not blindly carry forward an old rule.</strong>
              Marking rules can be changed by the Commission. For the actual
              examination, follow the current advertisement and applicable
              rules.
            </div>

            <h2 id="screening">6. Is JPSC Prelims Used for Final Merit?</h2>

            <p>
              Under the 2021 rules, the Preliminary Examination is a
              <strong>screening test</strong>. The rules state that Preliminary
              marks of candidates who qualify for the Main Examination are not
              counted for determining final merit. citeturn0search13
            </p>

            <div class="screening">
              <div>
                <span>PRELIMS</span>
                <strong>Screening</strong>
                <p>Clear the Preliminary stage according to the applicable qualification criteria.</p>
              </div>
              <b>→</b>
              <div>
                <span>MAINS</span>
                <strong>Written Examination</strong>
                <p>Prepare for the papers that contribute to the subsequent selection process.</p>
              </div>
              <b>→</b>
              <div>
                <span>INTERVIEW</span>
                <strong>Final process</strong>
                <p>Follow the current recruitment rules for the complete selection framework.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="syllabus">7. Exam Pattern + Syllabus = Your Real Study Blueprint</h2>

            <p>
              Exam pattern tells you <strong>how the test is delivered</strong>.
              Syllabus tells you <strong>what can be tested</strong>. PYQs tell
              you <strong>how JPSC has actually tested it</strong>.
            </p>

            <div class="three">
              <div><b>EXAM PATTERN</b><span>Papers • marks • questions • time</span></div>
              <div><b>SYLLABUS</b><span>Subjects • topics • boundaries</span></div>
              <div><b>PYQs</b><span>Actual questions • trends • framing</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula">
              <strong>Pattern + Syllabus + PYQ = Practical JPSC Preparation Map</strong>
            </div>

            <h2>8. Why the Number of Questions Matters</h2>

            <p>
              When a paper contains 100 objective questions in two hours, you
              have an average of about <strong>72 seconds per question</strong>
              if the entire two-hour window is converted into question-solving
              time. citeturn0search12
            </p>

            <div class="time">
              <div><b>120 MINUTES</b><span>2 hours</span></div>
              <div><b>100 QUESTIONS</b><span>Published paper size</span></div>
              <div><b>≈72 SEC</b><span>Average time/question</span></div>
              <div><b>DECISION</b><span>Not every question deserves equal time.</span></div>
            </div>

            <p>
              This is only an average. In a real examination, some questions
              will take 15–30 seconds while difficult questions may take much
              longer. Therefore, your preparation should include question
              selection, option elimination and time discipline.
            </p>

            <h2 id="time">9. Time Management Strategy From the Pattern</h2>

            <div class="time-strategy">
              <div>
                <span>ROUND 1</span>
                <strong>Direct questions</strong>
                <p>Answer questions you can solve confidently and quickly.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>ROUND 2</span>
                <strong>Reasoning / elimination</strong>
                <p>Return to questions where two or more options can be evaluated.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>ROUND 3</span>
                <strong>Difficult questions</strong>
                <p>Use remaining time for questions requiring deeper thought.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>FINAL</span>
                <strong>Review</strong>
                <p>Check marked questions and ensure no accidental multiple selection.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2>10. Accuracy vs Attempt Strategy</h2>

            <p>
              Do not build your strategy around a generic “attempt X questions”
              rule. Your ideal attempt count depends on the current marking
              rules, question difficulty, your accuracy and the nature of the
              paper.
            </p>

            <div class="decision">
              <div><b>KNOW IT</b><span>Answer confidently.</span></div>
              <div><b>CAN ELIMINATE</b><span>Use evidence and option logic.</span></div>
              <div><b>UNCERTAIN</b><span>Apply the current marking rule before deciding.</span></div>
              <div><b>NO BASIS</b><span>Do not turn every question into a random gamble.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>11. How the Pattern Changes Your Preparation</h2>

            <div class="changes">
              <div><b>Objective paper</b><span>Concept recall must become fast recognition.</span></div>
              <div><b>Two papers</b><span>Prepare both instead of treating one as optional.</span></div>
              <div><b>100 questions</b><span>Practise sustained MCQ decision-making.</span></div>
              <div><b>Two hours</b><span>Train under timed conditions.</span></div>
              <div><b>Broad GS</b><span>Build breadth without abandoning conceptual clarity.</span></div>
              <div><b>Jharkhand focus</b><span>Maintain a dedicated state-specific revision system.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="pyq">12. Use PYQs to Understand the Pattern</h2>

            <p>
              A paper pattern becomes useful only when you see how questions
              behave inside it. Solve previous papers under timed conditions,
              then classify every question by subject, topic, difficulty and
              error type.
            </p>

            <div class="pyq-cycle">
              <div><b>1</b><span>Attempt</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>2</b><span>Check</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>3</b><span>Map topic</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>4</b><span>Analyse error</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>5</b><span>Revise</span></div>
              <i>→</i>
              <div><b>6</b><span>Retest</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="conversion">
              <div>
                <span>HABITAT IAS • JPSC PYQ</span>
                <h2>Understand the pattern through actual questions.</h2>
                <p>
                  Use topic-wise PYQs to see which parts of the syllabus turn
                  into questions and how they are framed.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <a class="btn yellow" href="/book/">Explore JPSC PYQ Book →</a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="mock">13. Use Mock Tests to Reproduce the Pattern</h2>

            <p>
              After studying the syllabus and PYQs, full-length mocks should
              reproduce the practical conditions of the applicable examination:
              fixed time, objective questions, rapid decision-making and
              post-test analysis.
            </p>

            <div class="mock">
              <div><span>BEFORE</span><strong>Set timer</strong><p>Replicate the applicable paper duration.</p></div>
              <div><span>DURING</span><strong>Attempt strategically</strong><p>Practise question selection and time control.</p></div>
              <div><span>AFTER</span><strong>Analyse</strong><p>Find knowledge, concept and careless errors.</p></div>
              <div><span>THEN</span><strong>Revise</strong><p>Repair the weak syllabus topics.</p></div>
            </div>

            <div class="conversion">
              <div>
                <span>HABITAT IAS • JPSC MOCK TEST</span>
                <h2>Now test your preparation.</h2>
                <p>
                  Attempt the free mock and use the result as a diagnostic,
                  rather than treating the score as the end of preparation.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="cta-buttons">
                <a class="btn yellow" href="/mock-tests/free-gs/">Attempt Free Mock →</a>
                <a class="btn outline" href="/mock-tests/">View Mock Tests →</a>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2>14. JPSC Exam Pattern and Jharkhand-Specific Preparation</h2>

            <p>
              Paper-I includes a Jharkhand-specific component in the published
              2024 advertisement. That means your preparation should not treat
              Jharkhand as a last-minute appendix to general studies. citeturn0search12
            </p>

            <div class="jharkhand">
              <div><b>HISTORY</b><span>Movements, resistance, regional developments and personalities.</span></div>
              <div><b>GEOGRAPHY</b><span>Districts, rivers, plateau, forests, minerals and industries.</span></div>
              <div><b>SOCIETY</b><span>Tribes, communities and social characteristics.</span></div>
              <div><b>CULTURE</b><span>Languages, literature, festivals, art, music and heritage.</span></div>
              <div><b>ECONOMY</b><span>Mining, industry, agriculture, infrastructure and development.</span></div>
              <div><b>CURRENT</b><span>Recent state developments linked with static knowledge.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>15. Common Mistakes About the Exam Pattern</h2>

            <div class="mistakes">
              <div><b>01</b><span>Using a coaching website's old pattern as the final authority.</span></div>
              <div><b>02</b><span>Ignoring the second Preliminary paper.</span></div>
              <div><b>03</b><span>Memorising marks without checking the latest notice.</span></div>
              <div><b>04</b><span>Practising untimed MCQs only.</span></div>
              <div><b>05</b><span>Using an old negative-marking assumption without verification.</span></div>
              <div><b>06</b><span>Studying subjects but never practising complete papers.</span></div>
              <div><b>07</b><span>Ignoring Jharkhand-specific preparation.</span></div>
              <div><b>08</b><span>Focusing only on question count instead of accuracy and decision-making.</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2>16. A Better JPSC Preparation Formula</h2>

            <div class="formula-large">
              <div><b>OFFICIAL PATTERN</b><span>Know the rules</span></div>
              <strong>+</strong>
              <div><b>SYLLABUS</b><span>Know the boundaries</span></div>
              <strong>+</strong>
              <div><b>PYQs</b><span>Know the demand</span></div>
              <strong>+</strong>
              <div><b>MOCKS</b><span>Know your performance</span></div>
              <strong>=</strong>
              <div><b>EXAM READINESS</b><span>Close the gaps</span></div>
            </div>

            <h2 id="plan">17. 30-Day Pattern-Based Preparation Plan</h2>

            <div class="plan">
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 1–5</span>
                <strong>Pattern + syllabus</strong>
                <p>Read the official documents and create your master checklist.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 6–12</span>
                <strong>PYQs</strong>
                <p>Map previous questions subject-wise and topic-wise.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 13–19</span>
                <strong>Concept repair</strong>
                <p>Study weak topics revealed by PYQ analysis.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 20–25</span>
                <strong>Timed papers</strong>
                <p>Practise complete papers under the applicable time limit.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>DAYS 26–30</span>
                <strong>Mocks + revision</strong>
                <p>Test, analyse, revise and retest.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <h2>18. Official JPSC Sources</h2>

            <p>
              JPSC's official syllabus page currently lists the Combined Civil
              Services Examination syllabus. The Commission's recruitment
              records also list the 2025 Combined Civil Services Examination
              under Advt. No. 01/2026, along with the relevant examination rules
              and notices. citeturn0search9turn0search6
            </p>

            <div class="official">
              <a href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                JPSC Official Website →
              </a>
              <a href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/Syllabus.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                JPSC Official Syllabus →
              </a>
              <a href="https://www.jpsc.gov.in/exam_files.php?id=13039" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                JPSC 2025 Examination Documents →
              </a>
            </div>

            <h2>19. Final Takeaway</h2>

            <div class="final">
              <div><b>2 PAPERS</b><span>Prepare both.</span></div>
              <div><b>200 + 200</b><span>Know the marks.</span></div>
              <div><b>100 + 100</b><span>Train for MCQs.</span></div>
              <div><b>2 + 2 HOURS</b><span>Practise under time.</span></div>
              <div><b>PYQ + MOCK</b><span>Convert knowledge into performance.</span></div>
            </div>

            <p>
              The exam pattern should not be a fact you memorise once. It should
              determine how you study, how you practise, how you manage time and
              how you analyse your mocks. Keep the official notification as your
              final authority, then use PYQs and mocks to turn that pattern into
